WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.472
Firmware is long-lived software that often
runs close to hardware,

2
00:00:03.552 --> 00:00:07.149
controls physical behavior, processes
device input, stores identity material,

3
00:00:07.229 --> 00:00:08.741
and remains in the field for years.

4
00:00:08.821 --> 00:00:11.532
A firmware decision may affect not only a
process or a service,

5
00:00:11.612 --> 00:00:13.801
but a device that is deployed, serviced,
updated,

6
00:00:13.881 --> 00:00:16.123
and recovered outside the developer's
workspace.

7
00:00:16.203 --> 00:00:18.236
Rust gives firmware teams strong
advantages.

8
00:00:18.316 --> 00:00:21.705
Ownership and borrowing make buffer
responsibility easier to reason about.

9
00:00:21.785 --> 00:00:23.662
Type safety helps represent valid states.

10
00:00:23.742 --> 00:00:27.443
Safe Rust reduces many memory-corruption
risks that have historically mattered in

11
00:00:27.523 --> 00:00:28.357
embedded systems.

12
00:00:28.437 --> 00:00:29.584
Those strengths are real,

13
00:00:29.664 --> 00:00:32.584
and they are a major reason Rust is
attractive for device software.

14
00:00:32.664 --> 00:00:35.062
But secure firmware still requires
defensive design.

15
00:00:35.142 --> 00:00:38.218
Rust does not automatically decide whether
an update process is safe,

16
00:00:38.298 --> 00:00:39.914
whether debug access is controlled,

17
00:00:39.994 --> 00:00:42.079
whether hardware assumptions are
documented,

18
00:00:42.159 --> 00:00:44.557
whether protocol input is valid for the
current state,

19
00:00:44.637 --> 00:00:46.983
or whether secrets are exposed through
diagnostics.

20
00:00:47.063 --> 00:00:50.035
Language safety is one layer, not the
whole product security model.

21
00:00:50.115 --> 00:00:52.357
Secure firmware in Rust is a lifecycle
discipline.

22
00:00:52.437 --> 00:00:55.565
Code, hardware, build pipeline,
provisioning, deployment, updates,

23
00:00:55.645 --> 00:00:58.460
logging, diagnostics, field support, and
recovery all matter.

24
00:00:58.540 --> 00:01:02.033
A design that is elegant in source code
can still be fragile if release artifacts

25
00:01:02.113 --> 00:01:04.563
are hard to trace or field recovery
behavior is unclear.

26
00:01:04.643 --> 00:01:07.823
The defensive goal is firmware with
explicit, reviewable assumptions.

27
00:01:07.903 --> 00:01:10.614
Engineers should be able to explain what
the firmware trusts,

28
00:01:10.694 --> 00:01:13.927
what it validates, what resources are
bounded, how hardware is accessed,

29
00:01:14.007 --> 00:01:16.874
how updates are accepted or rejected, how
secrets are protected,

30
00:01:16.954 --> 00:01:19.300
and how the device behaves when something
goes wrong.
